Run the model on a NeuronCore with **native TorchNeuron** in `torch.bfloat16`: put weights, caches and inputs on `torch.device("neuron")` and compile the hot path with `torch.compile(fn, backend="neuron", fullgraph=True)`. Eager mode works on `neuron` tensors (each op compiles once per shape) and is fine for setup and correctness checks, but a decode loop must run as compiled regions. Do NOT run inference on CPU or in `float32` — it will be far too slow, and a CPU fallback is incorrect for this target. In eager mode an op without a Neuron kernel silently runs on CPU; check `torch_neuronx.get_fallback_ops()` after a request. There are no dynamic shapes: every new input shape is a compile that takes seconds to minutes, so pad to a small ladder of **static buckets**, compile every bucket at startup, and keep positions and lengths as tensors, not Python ints. The NEFF/HLO/NKI compile caches persist across rounds. Keep the KV cache **resident on the device and update it in place** inside the compiled step (in-place writes to an input tensor are supported); sample on device and read back only token ids. The container has no `torch_xla`, `torch_neuronx.trace` or NxD Inference — guidance that uses them describes the retired stack. FP8 matmuls need Trainium3: on trn2, dequantize FP8 checkpoints to BF16 at load. See the `neuron-pytorch`, `static-kv-cache`, `attention`, `tensor-parallel` and `nki-kernels` serving-systems references.
